To find the coordinates at which two linear equations intersect, we need to solve the system of equations simultaneously. 

The given equations are:
\begin{align*}
9x-4y&=30 \quad \text{(Equation 1)} \\
7x+y&=11 \quad \text{(Equation 2)}
\end{align*}

We can solve this system by using either substitution or elimination method. Let's use the elimination method in this case.

Multiply Equation 2 by 4 to make the coefficients of $y$ in both equations the same:
\begin{align*}
4(7x+y)&=4(11) \\
28x+4y&=44 \quad \text{(Equation 3)}
\end{align*}

Now, subtract Equation 1 from Equation 3 to eliminate $y$:
\begin{align*}
(28x+4y)-(9x-4y)&=44-30 \\
28x+4y-9x+4y&=14 \\
19x+8y&=14 \quad \text{(Equation 4)}
\end{align*}

Now we have two equations with only $x$ and $y$ variables:
\begin{align*}
9x-4y&=30 \quad \text{(Equation 1)} \\
19x+8y&=14 \quad \text{(Equation 4)}
\end{align*}

To eliminate $y$, we can multiply Equation 1 by 2 and Equation 4 by 1:
\begin{align*}
2(9x-4y)&=2(30) \\
18x-8y&=60 \quad \text{(Equation 5)} \\
1(19x+8y)&=1(14) \\
19x+8y&=14 \quad \text{(Equation 6)}
\end{align*}

Now, add Equation 5 and Equation 6 to eliminate $y$:
\begin{align*}
(18x-8y)+(19x+8y)&=60+14 \\
18x+19x-8y+8y&=74 \\
37x&=74 \\
x&=\frac{74}{37} \\
x&=2
\end{align*}

Substitute $x=2$ back into Equation 1 to find $y$:
\begin{align*}
9(2)-4y&=30 \\
18-4y&=30 \\
-4y&=30-18 \\
-4y&=12 \\
y&=\frac{12}{-4} \\
y&=-3
\end{align*}

Therefore, the coordinates where the two linear equations intersect are $(2,-3)$.

So, the answer is $\boxed{(2,-3)}$. The answer is: (2,-3)

