We can rewrite the given equation as 
\begin{align*}
e^{i \phi} + e^{i \delta} &= \cos(\phi) + i \sin(\phi) + \cos(\delta) + i \sin(\delta) \\
&= (\cos(\phi) + \cos(\delta)) + i (\sin(\phi) + \sin(\delta)).
\end{align*}

Since we have an equation involving real and imaginary parts, we can equate them separately. Equating the real parts, we have
\begin{align*}
\cos(\phi) + \cos(\delta) &= -\frac{5}{6}.
\end{align*}

Equating the imaginary parts, we have
\begin{align*}
\sin(\phi) + \sin(\delta) &= -\frac{1}{3}.
\end{align*}

Now, let's consider the equation for $e^{-i \phi} + e^{-i \delta}.$ We can rewrite it as 
\begin{align*}
e^{-i \phi} + e^{-i \delta} &= \cos(-\phi) + i \sin(-\phi) + \cos(-\delta) + i \sin(-\delta) \\
&= (\cos(\phi) - \cos(\delta)) - i (\sin(\phi) - \sin(\delta)).
\end{align*}

Thus, the real part of the expression is $\cos(\phi) - \cos(\delta)$ and the imaginary part is $-(\sin(\phi) - \sin(\delta)).$

Substituting the equations we obtained earlier, we have
\begin{align*}
\cos(\phi) - \cos(\delta) &= -\left(\frac{5}{6}\right), \\
-(\sin(\phi) - \sin(\delta)) &= \frac{1}{3}.
\end{align*}

Hence, $e^{-i \phi} + e^{-i \delta} = -\frac{5}{6} + \frac{1}{3} i.$

Therefore, the final answer is $\boxed{-\frac{5}{6} + \frac{1}{3} i}.$ The answer is: -\frac{5}{6}+\frac{1}{3}i

